<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Some concepts from Calculus I</title>

  <p>
    Here are a few ideas that appeared in Calculus I and that we'd
    like to review.  It's OK if you don't remember everything, but
    let's work together to help everyone feel caught up.
  </p>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's begin by remembering the relationship between velocity
        and distance traveled.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose you drive in your car at 70mph for 3 hours.  How far
          will you travel?  If you know your speed and the amount of
          time you travel at that speed, what is the general rule for
          determining the distance traveled?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          You travel <m>70\times 3 = 210</m> miles.  In general,
          distance equals velocity times time.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you take a trip in your car and measure your speed
        for 30 seconds:</p>
        <p>
          <tabular>
            <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Time (seconds)</cell>
              <cell>0</cell>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>15</cell>
              <cell>20</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>30</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Speed (feet/second)</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>35</cell>
              <cell>45</cell>
              <cell>50</cell>
              <cell>60</cell>
              <cell>70</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </p>
        
        <p>
          Estimate how far you travel during the first five seconds.  There
          are a couple of ways you could do this so write one sentence that
          explains what you did and share it with your group mates.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          At the start of the interval, we are traveling at 10 feet
          per second so let's assume we travel at 10 feet for second
          for 5 seconds.  This would be 50 feet.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate how far you have traveled over the entire 30-second
          interval.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          We will estimate the distance over each 5-second interval
          and add them together:
          <md>
            10\cdot5 + 25\cdot5 + 35\cdot5 + 45\cdot5 + 50\cdot5 +
            60\cdot5 = 1125 \text{feet}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Estimate your average speed over the 30-second interval.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          We find average speed as distance divided by time so
          <md>
            1125/30 = 37.5 \text{feet per second}
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.25in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>f</m> is a function satisfying <m>f(1)=2</m>,
        <m>f'(1)=1/2</m> and <m>f''(1)\lt 0</m>.
        Sketch a possible graph for <m>f</m> on the left below.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="30% 30%" margins="10% 10%">
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            An empty coordinate grid
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="01-empty-1-a">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/01-empty-1.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            An empty coordinate grid
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="01-empty-1-b">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/01-empty-1.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
        
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>g</m> is a function with <m>g'(2)=-2</m> and
        <m>g''(2)&gt;0</m>.  Sketch a possible graph for <m>g</m> on
        the right above.
      </p> 
      <p>
        Suppose that a function <m>h</m> satisfies <m>h(3)=4</m> and
        <m>h'(3)=2</m>.  Using this information, estimate
        <m>h(3.2)</m>.  What would knowledge of
        <m>h''(3)</m> tell you about your estimate?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="aaa-solutions">
      <sidebyside widths="30% 30%" margins="10% 10%">
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of a function satisfying the stated criteria
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="01-solution-1">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/01-solution-1.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of a function satisfying the stated criteria
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="01-solution-2">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/01-solution-2.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
      <p>
        <md>
          h(3.2)\approx h(3) + h'(3)(3.2-3) = 4 + 2(0.2)=4.4
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Shown below is the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="40%">
        <shortdescription>
          The graph of a function consisting of straight lines
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="01-graph-2">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/01-graph-2.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        Evaluate the definite integrals:
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^1 f(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          Look at the area under the curve between <m>x=0</m> and
          <m>x=1/2</m>.  This says that
          <md>
            \int_0^1 f(x)~dx = 1/2
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_1^3 f(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          Look at the area under the curve between <m>x=0</m> and
          <m>x=1/2</m>.  This says that
          <md>
            \int_1^3 f(x)~dx = 5/2
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>      
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^3 f(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>
            \int_0^3 f(x)~dx = 1/2+5/2=3
          </m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^4 f(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>
            \int_0^4 f(x)~dx = 3-1=2
          </m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^5 f(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>
            \int_0^5 f(x)~dx = 2-3/2 = 1/2
          </m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_2^5 f(x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>
            \int_2^5 f(x)~dx = \int_0^5 f(x)~dx - \int_0^2f(x)~dx =
            1/2 - 2 = -3/2
          </m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Evaluate the definite integrals using the Fundamental Theorem of
        Calculus:
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_{-1}^3 (3x^2+x)~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>\int_{-1}^3 (3x^2+x)~dx = (x^3+1/2x^2)|_{-1}^3 = 32</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\int_0^\pi \sin x~dx</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^\pi \sin x~dx = -\cos x|_0^\pi = 1-(-1)=2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^2 e^x~dx</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^2 e^x~dx = e^x|_0^2 = e^2-1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
